<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Wendel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sankt Wendel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. Wendel is a town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, named after Saint Wendelin, an early medieval hermit whose shrine in its Gothic basilica drew pilgrims. From 1816 to 1834 it was the capital of the Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> Principality of Lichtenberg, where unrest in 1832 led to military intervention, and after the principality's sale to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> it became a Prussian district town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-wendel.jpg" alt="St. Wendel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pimvantend · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sankt-Wendel-Schlossplatz.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The centre of the town was supposedly the farm of a lord named Baso from the Merovingian period, in the late 6th century, so the place was first called Basonevillare, the farm of Baso. In the mid-7th century Bishop Paulus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> bought it, and the area belonged to Verdun for centuries. The hermit Wendelin died near the settlement shortly before. He was highly venerated, an intense pilgrimage developed, and in the 12th century the settlement was renamed St. Wendel. A church was built on the site of today's basilica in the late 9th or early 10th century, and Wendelin's relics were taken there in the 11th century.</p><p>In 1326/28 Archbishop Balduin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, prince elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, bought the castle and village, which soon became a medieval town, and the emperor Ludwig IV granted its city certificate in 1332. A wall was built in 1388, when about 500 people lived there.</p><p>The town suffered repeated destruction. A fire in 1591 destroyed a large part of it, requisitions in the Thirty Years' War almost bankrupted it, and in 1677 all but a few buildings were burned down. Only from the middle of the 18th century did it grow again, with a wool and leather industry and a baroque dome on the basilica. From 1798 the canton belonged to the French Saardepartement.</p><p>In 1814 Duke Ernst III of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> received the cantons of St. Wendel, Grumbach and Baumholder, about 20,000 people, and called the area the Principality of Lichtenberg from 1816. Dissatisfaction with his rule led to uprisings in 1832, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hambach-festival">Hambach Festival</a>, which were put down with the help of Prussian troops from Saarlouis. In 1834 the duke sold the land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and the town became a chief town of an administrative district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> with a garrison.</p><p>The railway between Bingen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a> opened in 1860 and brought a train maintenance works. St. Wendel, Breiten and Alsfassen were united as one town in 1859. After World War II Saarland remained a French protectorate until it rejoined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> in 1957, and a French garrison stayed in the town from 1951 to 1999.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>St. Wendel lies on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blies">Blies</a>, 36 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a>, and has about 26,000 residents since a district reform of 1974 joined several surrounding villages to it. Pilgrims still come to the Wendalinus basilica at the beginning of October. The International Sculpture Symposium, begun in 1971 by the local sculptor Leo Kornbrust, produced stone sculptures that since 1979 line 25 kilometres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a> hiking trail from St. Wendel to Lake Bostal. The town's coat of arms uses four lilies from the Scottish royal arms, recalling the legend that Wendelin was a Scottish king's son.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sankt Wendel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1816 he received a district around <b>St. Wendel</b> on the upper Nahe and Blies, far from Coburg, which was named the Principality of Lichtenberg in 1819 after the castle near Kusel.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-lichtenberg">Principality of Lichtenberg</a></li></ul></section>
